 \label{subsec:ablation-results}
-Table~\ref{tab:ablations} shows the ablation results. The median RMSE of the WPINN is $8.13\times10^{-5}$, and three of the five seeds are below $10^{-4}$. Without causal weights the median is about 2.3 times larger, but the difference is not significant (Mann-Whitney $p=0.196$), and for seed 2 the no-causal run has lower RMSE than the WPINN. Without warm start, all three seeds have larger RMSE than the WPINN with the same seed. The mean Adam steps also rise to 102,989, and 17-19 of the 108 stages reach the cap, while the WPINN has only 1-2. When one network is reused for all windows, the RMSE is between 0.350 and 0.648. With separate networks and the same no-causal schedule it is between $1.75\times10^{-4}$ and $5.16\times10^{-4}$. This matches what we noticed in Section~III, that the previous learning is lost or degraded when one network moves to a new window. The causal single network gives 0.275 in its one run. This is lower than the full-interval run but still far from the windowed runs. These runs support the combination of windows, warm start and causal weights that we tested for this orbit. But with three to five seeds, they do not show that causal weighting always improves the accuracy.
+Table~\ref{tab:ablations} shows the ablation results. WPINN has median RMSE $8.13\times10^{-5}$, with three of five seeds below $10^{-4}$. Without causal weights the median is about 2.3 times larger. An exact one-sided Mann--Whitney U test compares no-causal seeds 1--3 with WPINN seeds 0--4, testing for larger no-causal errors ($U=11$, $p=0.196$). This comparison is exploratory given the small groups and shared seed identifiers; seed 2 has lower error without causal weights. Without warm start, all three matched seeds have larger errors, mean Adam updates rise to 102,989, and 17--19 stages reach the cap, compared with 1--2 for WPINN. Reusing one network over the windows gives RMSEs of 0.350--0.648, compared with $1.75\times10^{-4}$--$5.16\times10^{-4}$ for separate networks under the same no-causal schedule. These final errors favor separate networks, but do not measure when earlier-window accuracy changes during training. The causal-single run gives RMSE 0.275. Together, the saved runs support the windowed training structure and the training-cost benefit of warm start for this orbit, without establishing a consistent accuracy gain from causal weighting.
 
 \section{Conclusion}
 
-Our 27-window PINN passes every check on the nearly closed Lorenz-system interval $[0,13.26446]$. The seed-0 run had a combined state RMSE of $6.97\times10^{-5}$ in comparison to DOP853, and the median over five seeds is $8.13\times10^{-5}$. A separate network trained on the full interval recorded an RMSE of 2.237. This research applies to only one initial state and one coefficient set. Other initial states and other coefficient sets may result in different behavior. Future research will focus on matched configurations and initial states and generalize to problems with specified boundary conditions at each end.
+Our 27-window PINN approximates the nearly closed Lorenz-system orbit on $[0,13.26446]$ with a five-seed median combined RMSE of $8.13\times10^{-5}$ against DOP853. The seed-0 RMSE is $6.97\times10^{-5}$, compared with 2.237 for the saved full-interval network. Separate window networks give lower final errors under the compared settings, and warm start reduces Adam updates while improving the matched-seed accuracy. This provides an effective training structure for the studied initial-value problem. Future work will test matched configurations, other initial states and coefficient sets to assess how well the approach extends beyond this orbit.
 
 
 
